WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.134
A posthumous birth is when a baby is born after the death of his or her parent.

00:00:03.172 --> 00:00:06.090
It's something that's almost sure to have psychological implications

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:09.626
for both the child and the surviving parent, as we've seen before in

00:00:09.658 --> 00:00:12.602
Stephen King's Carrie, in the strained relationship between Carrie and her mother.

00:00:12.666 --> 00:00:15.566
Another example is A Nightmare on Elm Street 5 with Alice and her son Jacob.

00:00:15.588 --> 00:00:18.718
But there is perhaps no darker an example of the effects of a

00:00:18.724 --> 00:00:22.042
posthumous birth than in the 2014 horror film The Babadook.

00:00:22.106 --> 00:00:25.314
The protagonist, a mother named Amelia, loses her husband in a car wreck

00:00:25.362 --> 00:00:27.362
just before giving birth to her son Samuel.

00:00:27.426 --> 00:00:31.046
But something else is born that day a monster that would become known as The Babadook.

00:00:31.068 --> 00:00:34.838
To learn about the origins of this icon of modern horror and the

00:00:34.844 --> 00:00:38.506
part of the ending that nobody's talking about, stick around to the end of

00:00:38.528 --> 00:00:39.194
this video.

00:00:39.312 --> 00:00:41.610
This video is sponsored by Factor.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:47.278
♪ Metal Music ♪

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:49.674
Life is not always as it seems.

00:00:49.792 --> 00:00:53.670
It can be a wondrous thing, but it can also be very treacherous.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:54.826
Welcome to Horror History.

00:00:54.928 --> 00:00:58.174
If you're watching this video, this Horror History lesson, then you probably

00:00:58.212 --> 00:01:00.010
already know what The Babadook represents.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.942
So let's just cut the !@#$ and skip to my conclusion at the end of the video

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:05.434
where I tell you that The Babadook represents the grief of the struggling

00:01:05.482 --> 00:01:06.234
mother, Amelia.

00:01:06.282 --> 00:01:08.554
I think everyone already kinda knows that about this movie.

00:01:08.602 --> 00:01:11.438
It's not gonna be a groundbreaking revelation when I say that at the end of

00:01:11.444 --> 00:01:13.370
the video, nobody's mind is gonna be blown.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.414
Oh my God, I never looked at it like that.

00:01:15.452 --> 00:01:16.870
I'm going to subscribe to this guy.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.926
Nah. What I'm more interested for this video is how The Babadook is the

00:01:19.948 --> 00:01:23.286
embodiment of grief, from the way he behaves, to his appearance, to the

00:01:23.308 --> 00:01:26.134
secret lore that you can only get if you have the physical copy of the

00:01:26.172 --> 00:01:28.022
Mister Babadook book, the dook book.

00:01:28.076 --> 00:01:31.414
The movie is actually an adaptation of writer-director Jennifer Kent's

00:01:31.452 --> 00:01:32.450
2005 short film Monster.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.958
It similarly features a single mom of a child named Samuel who is obsessed

00:01:35.974 --> 00:01:36.870
with fighting monsters.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.350
The mother eventually starts seeing the horror for herself.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:42.506
In a 2014 interview with The Cut, Jennifer Kent explained that this

00:01:42.528 --> 00:01:45.486
character was inspired by a friend of hers who was a single mother whose son

00:01:45.508 --> 00:01:48.286
was traumatized by a monster figure he thought he saw everywhere in the house,

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:52.697
causing Jennifer to imagine what would happen if the monster was real on some level.

00:01:52.697 --> 00:01:54.586
The monster in the original short film is not named,

00:01:54.618 --> 00:01:58.530
but the one in The Babadook is, in case you haven't figured that out.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.486
While speaking to The Dissolve, Kent said she was staying with a Serbian

00:02:01.518 --> 00:02:05.506
writer and asked him what's Serbian for boogeyman? He said Babaroga, but

00:02:05.528 --> 00:02:06.866
that didn't sound quite right to her.

00:02:06.888 --> 00:02:09.638
So she changed it to Babadook and it just felt right.

00:02:09.724 --> 00:02:11.330
But I think it sounds f!@#ing stupid.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.266
As we know, The Babadook represents grief.

00:02:13.298 --> 00:02:17.394
And grief is often experienced in five stages denial, anger, bargaining,

00:02:17.442 --> 00:02:18.642
depression and acceptance.

00:02:18.706 --> 00:02:21.794
This is known as the Kübler-Ross model, named after the Swiss psychiatrist

00:02:21.842 --> 00:02:23.834
Elizabeth Kübler-Ross, who identified it.

00:02:23.872 --> 00:02:26.666
When I first set out to make this video, I assumed that each stage would

00:02:26.688 --> 00:02:30.266
present itself chronologically, but what I noticed was that Amelia would

00:02:30.288 --> 00:02:33.142
often go backwards and demonstrate symptoms of the first four stages

00:02:33.206 --> 00:02:34.026
multiple times.

00:02:34.128 --> 00:02:37.134
So I did some more reading and found out that Kübler-Ross never actually

00:02:37.172 --> 00:02:38.762
intended the stages to be linear.

00:02:38.826 --> 00:02:41.966
People can experience these aspects of grief at different times, and they

00:02:41.988 --> 00:02:43.678
don't happen in one particular order.

00:02:43.764 --> 00:02:46.834
But with that in mind, the first stage to present itself in the movie is denial.

00:02:46.872 --> 00:02:49.986
So to understand the journey of The Babadook, we're going to take it

00:02:50.008 --> 00:02:51.460
back to stage one.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.702
(IMPACT)

00:02:54.702 --> 00:02:58.390
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.858
It all began seven years before the events of the film, when Amelia's

00:03:01.874 --> 00:03:05.042
husband Oskar was killed while driving her to the hospital to give birth.

00:03:05.106 --> 00:03:07.414
Instead of facing her trauma, Amelia avoids it.

00:03:07.452 --> 00:03:10.454
She literally locks all of his stuff up in the basement and forbids her son

00:03:10.492 --> 00:03:14.026
from going inside, a representation of how she's locking away her grief and

00:03:14.048 --> 00:03:15.082
refusing to face it.

00:03:15.136 --> 00:03:18.154
She avoids talking about it and even gets upset when friends or family

00:03:18.192 --> 00:03:19.642
members broach the subject at all.

00:03:19.696 --> 00:03:21.974
This essentially gives birth to The Babadook.

00:03:22.022 --> 00:03:25.813
The Babadook metaphorically grows inside her the longer she tries to deny her trauma,

00:03:25.813 --> 00:03:28.718
but after years of doing this, it grows too large for her to

00:03:28.724 --> 00:03:30.862
keep in, and even Samuel takes note of it.

00:03:30.916 --> 00:03:34.286
In both Monster and The Babadook, Samuel becomes obsessed with killing the monster,

00:03:34.308 --> 00:03:37.294
with the former inventing homemade contraptions to fight back against it,

00:03:37.332 --> 00:03:39.770
like a dart gun and a catapult backpack.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.682
Both versions are obsessed with protecting their mother from the threat.

00:03:42.746 --> 00:03:45.686
It's interesting how the absence of a parent causes Sam to want to take on

00:03:45.708 --> 00:03:47.154
this man of the house kind of role.

00:03:47.202 --> 00:03:49.638
The short film version of The Monster is similar in appearance to

00:03:49.644 --> 00:03:51.110
The Babadook, with one key difference.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.054
The Babadook wears a top hat and a cape, much like Thurston the Great Magician,

00:03:54.092 --> 00:03:55.922
who Samuel sees as an idol.

00:03:55.986 --> 00:03:59.234
Sam is an aspiring magician himself, often dressing up like his hero,

00:03:59.282 --> 00:04:02.310
perhaps the closest thing that the boy has to a father figure in his life.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.498
It's likely no mistake that The Babadook takes on a similar form.

00:04:05.584 --> 00:04:08.826
Since Amelia's grief is centered around the loss of a man in the house,

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:11.830
A DVD of an Illusionist is no proper replacement for a dad.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.974
Every year around the time of Samuel's birthday, Amelia's anxiety gets worse.

00:04:15.012 --> 00:04:17.838
In psychology, this is known as the anniversary effect.

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:21.146
This article from the American Psychological Association lists a few healthy

00:04:21.178 --> 00:04:24.186
coping strats, like recognizing your feelings, finding healthy outlets,

00:04:24.218 --> 00:04:26.862
remembering and celebrating the lives of loved ones, and using your support system.

00:04:26.916 --> 00:04:30.242
But since Amelia is still in denial, she doesn't do any of these

00:04:30.296 --> 00:04:31.410
except for the last one.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.866
Each year, they do a joint birthday party for Samuel and his cousin Ruby,

00:04:34.888 --> 00:04:38.270
So that Amelia doesn't have to celebrate on the actual day of Oskar's death.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:41.702
A couple weeks before Samuel's 7th birthday, those feelings rise up again,

00:04:41.756 --> 00:04:44.354
but Ruby no longer wants to do a joint birthday party.

00:04:44.402 --> 00:04:47.478
This essentially eliminates Amelia's last line of defense from having to

00:04:47.484 --> 00:04:50.822
face her trauma, and as a result, The Babadook manifests in the real world

00:04:50.876 --> 00:04:53.242
for the first time in the form of a book called Mr. Babadook.

00:04:53.296 --> 00:04:57.066
When Samuel chooses it as a bedtime story, Amelia doesn't know

00:04:57.088 --> 00:04:58.698
what it is or where it came from.

00:04:58.784 --> 00:05:02.902
-[Amelia] If it's in a word or it's in a look, you can't get rid of The Babadook.

00:05:02.966 --> 00:05:04.788
The text goes on to say

00:05:04.788 --> 00:05:07.851
-[Amelia] If you're a really clever one and you know what it is to see, 

00:05:07.851 --> 00:05:12.173
-[Amelia] Then you can make friends with a special one, a friend of you and me.

00:05:12.173 --> 00:05:15.982
-[Amelia] His name is Mr. Babadook and this is his book.

00:05:16.116 --> 00:05:19.346
This is telling us that it takes a special person, or a person with a lot of resilience,

00:05:19.368 --> 00:05:21.986
to learn to live with the grief inside of them, which is what

00:05:22.008 --> 00:05:23.406
Amelia has to do in this movie.

00:05:23.438 --> 00:05:26.286
It goes on to explain that The Babadook can be identified by a rumbling

00:05:26.318 --> 00:05:29.218
sound and three sharp knocks, followed by the croaking of its name.

00:05:29.304 --> 00:05:32.850
The book also hints at a much more terrifying true form of The Babadook.

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:36.326
See him in your room at night and you won't sleep a wink I'll soon take off

00:05:36.348 --> 00:05:39.746
my funny disguise take heed of what you've read and once you see what's

00:05:39.778 --> 00:05:42.386
underneath, you're going to wish you were dead.

00:05:42.498 --> 00:05:46.058
Samuel is terrified, causing Amelia to put the book away high on top of a dresser,

00:05:46.064 --> 00:05:49.126
much like how she puts her difficult to face feelings out of sight

00:05:49.158 --> 00:05:49.706
and mind.

00:05:49.808 --> 00:05:52.214
After this, Samuel's behavior continues to worsen.

00:05:52.262 --> 00:05:55.678
At first, it appears to be Sam's disobedience that stresses out his mom, but

00:05:55.684 --> 00:05:58.606
it would soon become apparent that his behavior is actually a result of

00:05:58.628 --> 00:06:01.486
Amelia's inability to manage her grief, which has an effect on her parenting. 

00:06:01.508 --> 00:06:04.494
When he gets in trouble at school, she insists that she'll talk to him,

00:06:04.532 --> 00:06:07.882
but we never see that happen, and she continues to avoid conflict.

00:06:07.946 --> 00:06:11.234
A coworker offers to cover for her at work, and instead of picking up her son,

00:06:11.272 --> 00:06:13.954
she takes some time to herself before going back to him.

00:06:13.992 --> 00:06:16.466
When she finally picks him up from her sister's house, she finds that he

00:06:16.488 --> 00:06:18.574
scared the crap out of Ruby with his antics.

00:06:18.702 --> 00:06:22.566
-[Ruby] Insist on talking to this bloody Babadook thing all day, just talking to the air.

00:06:22.588 --> 00:06:26.054
There's a confrontation when Samuel insists that The Babadook is real

00:06:26.092 --> 00:06:28.290
and defiantly spikes a firecracker on the ground.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:31.594
Distracted with her own wallowing, she's completely lost control of her own son. 

00:06:31.632 --> 00:06:34.262
-[Amelia] Where did you get those firecrackers?

00:06:34.262 --> 00:06:36.064
-[Sam] You got them for me on the Internet.

00:06:36.064 --> 00:06:37.334
-[Amelia] That's the end of the Internet.

00:06:37.334 --> 00:06:38.410
Okay, bye.

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:42.606
The ornery behavior continues, and in her frustration, Amelia begins to

00:06:42.628 --> 00:06:45.150
experience the second stage of grief... anger.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.115
(Sam Screaming)

00:06:47.115 --> 00:06:49.115
-[Amelia] Just be normal!

00:06:49.115 --> 00:06:51.619
(IMPACT)

