♪ Mysterious Music ♪

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:00.386
Amelia's rage is initially sparked when she catches her son with the key to

00:08:00.408 --> 00:08:02.562
the basement, where all of Oskar's old things are kept.

00:08:02.626 --> 00:08:05.686
As she tries to put things back in order, she's startled by his old hat and

00:08:05.708 --> 00:08:06.834
blazer on the coat rack.

00:08:06.882 --> 00:08:09.638
This shows that the appearance of The Babadook isn't just inspired by the magician,

00:08:09.644 --> 00:08:12.746
but that Oskar had a similar sense of style and the creature is

00:08:12.768 --> 00:08:14.150
filling the void of his absence.

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:16.634
Samuel's apparent transgressions begin to pile up.

00:08:16.672 --> 00:08:19.734
Amelia finds glass in her soup, finds the picture of her and Oskar

00:08:19.782 --> 00:08:23.034
vandalized with her eyes and mouth made to look kind of like The Babadook.

00:08:23.082 --> 00:08:26.094
And he even seems to get physically aggressive when she tries to take away

00:08:26.132 --> 00:08:27.945
his homemade monster fighting weapons.

00:08:29.422 --> 00:08:31.165
-[Sam] Do you wanna die?

00:08:31.263 --> 00:08:33.758
Fighting words from someone who is literally three and a

00:08:33.764 --> 00:08:35.614
half feet tall, but kind of based.

00:08:35.732 --> 00:08:38.370
The next night, he sees his wardrobe open on its own.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.622
We don't find out what happens, but there's a huge thud.

00:08:40.686 --> 00:08:43.522
Samuel screams, and his mom finds him hyperventilating under the bed,

00:08:43.576 --> 00:08:45.202
repeatedly saying, don't let it in.

00:08:45.256 --> 00:08:47.554
While it appears that he's worried about the monster getting into the house,

00:08:47.592 --> 00:08:50.886
as warned in the storybook, on a subtextual level, he's telling her not to

00:08:50.908 --> 00:08:54.150
let her dark side get in and take hold of her, something that has obviously

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:55.014
happened before.

00:08:55.132 --> 00:08:58.118
Still, not having accepted the existence of The Babadook, Amelia tries to

00:08:58.124 --> 00:09:00.774
rip up the book and throw it away, which is about as useful as putting

00:09:00.812 --> 00:09:01.810
Chucky in the trash.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:03.478
The horror always comes back.

00:09:03.564 --> 00:09:06.840
That night, she hears the three knocks, as specified in the book.

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:11.806
The toll that The Babadook is taking on her becomes apparent the next day at

00:09:11.828 --> 00:09:12.874
Ruby's birthday party.

00:09:12.922 --> 00:09:15.962
Of all of the moms at the party, she's the only one not wearing makeup.

00:09:16.026 --> 00:09:18.878
And while I don't find makeup to be necessary, I think the implication is

00:09:18.884 --> 00:09:21.422
that she's the only one who didn't have time to do it.

00:09:21.476 --> 00:09:25.038
Again, we see her anger slip out in unintended ways when one of the women says that

00:09:25.044 --> 00:09:27.840
she's been too busy to go to the gym because of her husband's business.

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:30.254
-[Claire] I don't even have time to go to the gym anymore.

00:09:30.302 --> 00:09:31.102
-[Claire] It's ridiculous.

00:09:31.166 --> 00:09:34.286
-[Amelia] That's a real tragedy, not having time to go to the gym anymore.

00:09:34.318 --> 00:09:37.746
-[Amelia] How do you cope? You must have so much to talk about with those poor,

00:09:37.778 --> 00:09:39.080
-[Amelia] disadvantaged women.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:42.323
Wait, where have I seen this before?

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:45.782
Yep, that's the one.

00:09:45.836 --> 00:09:48.486
Her outburst makes it seem as though Amelia probably hasn't had the

00:09:48.508 --> 00:09:51.970
opportunity to exercise in a long time, which is also probably connected to

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:53.430
her escalating grief symptoms.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:56.618
This meta analysis found that exercise during bereavement can have a

00:09:56.624 --> 00:09:58.282
positive effect on psychological health.

00:09:58.336 --> 00:10:01.146
And plus, I think we all know that, it's common sense that you feel better

00:10:01.168 --> 00:10:01.990
when you exercise.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:05.966
Meanwhile, Ruby bullies Samuel for not having a dad, antagonizing him by

00:10:05.988 --> 00:10:08.542
saying that his dad isn't around because he doesn't want him.

00:10:08.596 --> 00:10:10.298
This causes Sam to snap.

00:10:10.394 --> 00:10:11.840
-[Ruby] No one wants you.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:14.900
Welcome to the kill count.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:17.010
I'm so immature.

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:22.606
During the tantrum, on the car ride home, Sam starts screaming at an unseen

00:10:22.638 --> 00:10:24.050
entity, telling it to get out.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:26.914
It makes sense that he would be able to see The Babadook before her,

00:10:26.952 --> 00:10:28.818
because she has yet to accept that she's the problem.

00:10:28.904 --> 00:10:32.098
Sam starts convulsing in the backseat, which leads to an emergency doctor's visit,

00:10:32.114 --> 00:10:34.882
where it's concluded that he experienced a febrile convulsion,

00:10:34.946 --> 00:10:36.402
basically overheating of the brain.

00:10:36.466 --> 00:10:39.606
The doctor is concerned about his anxiety level, but sees the invention of a

00:10:39.628 --> 00:10:42.370
monster to be a perfectly normal thing for a child to imagine.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:46.038
However, we know that the monster is a metaphor for Amelia's grief, so it's

00:10:46.054 --> 00:10:49.210
really her mental issues that are causing harm and distress to her own son.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:50.602
A pretty sad situation.

00:10:50.736 --> 00:10:53.978
The doctor reluctantly supplies her with some sedatives to help Sam sleep at night,

00:10:53.984 --> 00:10:56.490
just until they can get an appointment with the psychiatrist.

00:10:56.570 --> 00:11:00.026
This helps her get a much needed rest until the next morning, when she hears

00:11:00.058 --> 00:11:03.342
the familiar three knocks on her door and looks down to discover

00:11:03.396 --> 00:11:05.034
the Mister Babadook book she had discarded,

00:11:05.082 --> 00:11:07.006
With all of the pages pasted back together.

00:11:07.108 --> 00:11:08.494
It's as the book forewarned.

00:11:08.542 --> 00:11:10.594
You can't get rid of The Babadook.

00:11:10.632 --> 00:11:13.282
This time when she flips through it, there are new pages added onto the end.

00:11:13.336 --> 00:11:15.298
I'll wager with you, I'll make you a bet.

00:11:15.384 --> 00:11:17.650
The more you deny, the stronger I get.

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:20.390
The next page shows The Babadook terrorizing her in the night.

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:22.054
You start to change when I get in.

00:11:22.092 --> 00:11:24.242
The Babadook growing right under your skin.

00:11:24.306 --> 00:11:26.498
Oh, come, come see what's underneath.

00:11:26.594 --> 00:11:29.698
This again, teases at the idea that what we've seen is not The Babadook's

00:11:29.714 --> 00:11:30.214
true form.

00:11:30.236 --> 00:11:32.386
The language seems to playfully reference the magician again.

00:11:32.418 --> 00:11:36.922
The oh, come see kind of reminds me of a sort of come one, come all.

00:11:36.976 --> 00:11:38.538
It's almost time to start the show.

00:11:38.624 --> 00:11:40.678
I should have done that at the beginning of this episode.

00:11:40.774 --> 00:11:42.186
I should do that in all my videos.

00:11:42.288 --> 00:11:45.454
Come one, come all to another episode of Horror History.

00:11:45.572 --> 00:11:48.042
All I need is like the nice Justin Whang mustache.

00:11:48.106 --> 00:11:51.018
In 2014, a limited number of these books were merchandised.

00:11:51.114 --> 00:11:54.142
The author is Jennifer Kent, which kind of suggests to me that the content

00:11:54.196 --> 00:11:55.322
of this book is canon.

00:11:55.386 --> 00:11:58.686
The movie does show some of these additional pages, but the full context is

00:11:58.708 --> 00:11:59.566
lost in the cut.

00:11:59.668 --> 00:12:01.354
There's just no way you're off the hook,

00:12:01.402 --> 00:12:04.718
If you're all grown up and you read this book, and you snub your nose

00:12:04.724 --> 00:12:08.050
with a civilized look, you'll appeal even more to the big Babadook.

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:10.918
The woman in the picture here appears to be Amelia, and this scene of the

00:12:10.924 --> 00:12:13.718
Babadook frightening her from above would eventually come to happen in the movie.

00:12:13.724 --> 00:12:16.598
The next page confirms what we already kinda figured out about the

00:12:16.604 --> 00:12:19.058
monster feeding off of her denial to gain strength.

00:12:19.154 --> 00:12:21.682
Then you'll be my puppet, my plaything, my pet.

00:12:21.746 --> 00:12:23.958
I'll make you do things you'll be sure to regret.

00:12:24.054 --> 00:12:27.066
To me, this is about the ability for her mental illness to take over her

00:12:27.088 --> 00:12:29.978
life and basically ruin things for her, which manifests in The Babadook as

00:12:29.984 --> 00:12:32.022
the power to possess and puppeteer the victim.

00:12:32.086 --> 00:12:35.926
The following pages show her choking the dog, then her son, before offing

00:12:35.958 --> 00:12:36.522
herself.

00:12:36.586 --> 00:12:39.738
There are more pages in the physical book that line up with more of Amelia's story,

00:12:39.754 --> 00:12:41.870
but I'll come back to that in a little bit.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:45.022
It's at this point that Amelia tries torching the book, but not long after,

00:12:45.076 --> 00:12:46.494
she receives an ominous phone call.

00:12:46.532 --> 00:12:48.080
Uttering the familiar words,

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:52.989
-[Babadook] Ba..ba..dook...dook.

00:12:52.989 --> 00:12:56.326
She even tries going to the police, but that's not really helpful seeing as

00:12:56.348 --> 00:12:57.874
how she just burned the only evidence

00:12:57.922 --> 00:13:00.758
and she ends up getting further spooked by this image of a hat and claws on

00:13:00.764 --> 00:13:02.774
the coat rack at the police station.

00:13:02.812 --> 00:13:05.122
At home, there are more signs that her sanity is starting to slip.

00:13:05.186 --> 00:13:07.766
The dog, Pugsy, barks at her and seems to be afraid of her now.

00:13:07.788 --> 00:13:10.998
The house is a mess and she discovers a cockroach infestation behind the fridge.

00:13:11.004 --> 00:13:14.074
And as all these problems are piling up, she's visited by state officials

00:13:14.112 --> 00:13:17.002
about the well being of her son, and they see that he's basically

00:13:17.056 --> 00:13:18.598
mellowed out on sleep medication.

00:13:18.694 --> 00:13:21.846
For the first time, she sees The Babadook with her own eyes,

00:13:21.878 --> 00:13:23.130
 momentarily standing in her neighbor's house.

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:26.334
This not only serves to make Amelia question her own sanity, but it shows

00:13:26.372 --> 00:13:28.042
that all of her problems are connected.

00:13:28.106 --> 00:13:31.038
Her mental state caused her to neglect cleaning the house, which led to the

00:13:31.044 --> 00:13:32.234
appearance of the cockroaches.

00:13:32.282 --> 00:13:33.706
The neighbor's name is Mrs. Roach,

00:13:33.738 --> 00:13:37.018
an elderly woman with Parkinson's disease, and The Babadook has

00:13:37.044 --> 00:13:38.110
cockroach like qualities.

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:41.842
It emits this hissing sound and in one dream it can be seen scuttling across

00:13:41.896 --> 00:13:43.214
the ceiling like a roach.

00:13:43.342 --> 00:13:46.574
The idea is that Amelia cannot compartmentalize her grief.

00:13:46.622 --> 00:13:49.880
If she doesn't address it, it's going to affect all aspects of her life.

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:55.426
After this dream, she wakes up Samuel and takes him downstairs, clearly

00:13:55.458 --> 00:13:57.186
starting to fear The Babadook as a real threat for the first time.

00:13:57.218 --> 00:14:00.450
She finds herself watching a strange old film on TV.

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:04.266
This is a real silent film from 1900 called The Magic Book, in which this

00:14:04.288 --> 00:14:06.970
magician brings the illustrations in this magic book to life.

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:10.458
It was directed by French director Georges Méliès, who started his career as

00:14:10.464 --> 00:14:12.662
a stage magician before getting involved in film.

00:14:12.726 --> 00:14:15.758
The version that Amelia watches, however, is altered and she sees

00:14:15.764 --> 00:14:19.386
The Babadook appear in that book and other old Méliès movie scenes, which keeps

00:14:19.418 --> 00:14:22.618
her awake all night, leaving her sick and exhausted the next morning and

00:14:22.644 --> 00:14:25.250
setting her up for stage four: depression.

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:29.041
(IMPACT)

00:14:30.844 --> 00:14:36.887
♪ Haunting Music ♪

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:40.934
Depression is characterized by loss of motivation, loss of interest and

00:14:40.972 --> 00:14:42.162
inability to feel pleasure.

00:14:42.226 --> 00:14:45.154
Amelia climbs into bed and ignores her responsibilities as a mother.

00:14:45.202 --> 00:14:48.246
Even when Samuel tells her that he feels sick from the sleeping pills and

00:14:48.268 --> 00:14:51.798
needs food, she sees his cries for help as an annoyance and lashes out at

00:14:51.804 --> 00:14:54.946
him with anger and after realizing what she's done, finds him in a fetal

00:14:54.978 --> 00:14:55.890
position on the floor.

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:58.934
She attempts to make up for it by taking him to his favorite restaurant, but

00:14:58.972 --> 00:15:02.126
the booth next to theirs is celebrating a birthday, a looming reminder about

00:15:02.148 --> 00:15:05.002
Sam's own birthday and the anniversary of Oskar's death.

00:15:05.066 --> 00:15:08.106
She decides to take him for a long drive afterwards, but suddenly sees

00:15:08.138 --> 00:15:10.958
Roaches in her lap and the image of The Babadook tailing their car.

00:15:11.044 --> 00:15:14.478
This causes her to swerve and crash the vehicle and in a panic runs from the

00:15:14.484 --> 00:15:15.290
scene of the incident.

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:17.438
She's spiraling further and further out of control.

00:15:17.524 --> 00:15:20.230
When she gets home, she plunges back into her depression,

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:21.490
 clinging to the things that comfort her.

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:25.058
She sits in the warm bathtub in her clothes, not doing anything, and goes to

00:15:25.064 --> 00:15:26.718
sleep with Oskar's old violin.

00:15:26.814 --> 00:15:29.326
Even Sam begins to fear her and attempts to call

00:15:29.358 --> 00:15:31.158
Mrs. Roach to see if he can stay over there instead.

00:15:31.244 --> 00:15:34.006
But when she catches him, she cuts the phone line to prevent him from

00:15:34.028 --> 00:15:35.298
embarrassing her any further.

00:15:35.394 --> 00:15:38.806
You know, Amelia, usually the bad guy is the one to do that, but go ahead,

00:15:38.828 --> 00:15:39.798
knock yourself out.

00:15:39.884 --> 00:15:42.518
Actually, it kinda tracks, considering that The Babadook is just a

00:15:42.524 --> 00:15:44.070
manifestation of her grief.

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:47.638
In a way, she is the bad guy of this story and the conflict is the internal

00:15:47.654 --> 00:15:49.190
struggle she's having against herself.

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:51.702
Her role is a lot like Jack Torrance from The Shining.

00:15:51.766 --> 00:15:54.846
Both characters started with good intentions, but are consumed by their

00:15:54.868 --> 00:15:56.410
afflictions to become the villain.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:59.646
And it wouldn't be long before the monster's promise of making her his

00:15:59.668 --> 00:16:01.210
puppet would become a reality.

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:04.478
She still insists that The Babadook isn't real, another clear cut example of

00:16:04.484 --> 00:16:07.450
her denial, and she makes a huge scene out of closing and locking all the windows.

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:11.166
The cycle of treating Sam with cruelty to pampering him continues

00:16:11.198 --> 00:16:13.886
through the night until she's lured out of the living room by the illusion

00:16:13.918 --> 00:16:15.890
of her son sleepwalking into the basement.

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:19.250
When she arrives there, she's shocked to see her late husband, Oskar. 

00:16:23.296 --> 00:16:26.166
Bargaining is described as the defense mechanism you go through to

00:16:26.188 --> 00:16:28.562
fight against the feeling of helplessness during a loss.

00:16:28.626 --> 00:16:31.686
It's essentially the series of what if scenarios you may go through in your

00:16:31.708 --> 00:16:34.154
head to try to cope with that which you cannot control.

00:16:34.272 --> 00:16:36.390
Maybe you've experienced this to some degree.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:39.946
If I hadn't said those things, maybe we wouldn't have broken up, or if I

00:16:39.968 --> 00:16:42.110
worked longer hours, they wouldn't have let me go.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:45.614
For Amelia, the bargaining stage happens in a very direct way, with her

00:16:45.652 --> 00:16:49.006
coming face to face with her deceased loved one and him literally making her an offer.

00:16:49.028 --> 00:16:55.414
-[Oskar] We can be together. You just need to bring me the boy. 

00:16:55.570 --> 00:16:59.346
The part of her subconscious responsible for seeing Oskar still feels that

00:16:59.368 --> 00:17:02.110
if they never had a child, she never would have lost her husband.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.582
But when she refuses to offer up her son, he recedes into the darkness,

00:17:05.646 --> 00:17:09.058
later emerging from the kitchen with the insectile hiss, scaring her away to

00:17:09.064 --> 00:17:09.630
the bedroom.

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:12.726
Wherever she goes, she's unable to avoid her trauma, and it comes down

00:17:12.748 --> 00:17:15.686
through the chimney, where it finally gets her and takes over her body.

00:17:15.788 --> 00:17:18.866
The book warned that it would make her do things she'd regret, and that's

00:17:18.898 --> 00:17:19.798
exactly what happened.

00:17:19.884 --> 00:17:23.106
The dog comes downstairs as she's watching a movie, and she chases him down

00:17:23.148 --> 00:17:24.758
and snaps his neck in the kitchen.

00:17:24.854 --> 00:17:27.994
She hasn't been a good mom up to this point, but this is by far the most

00:17:28.032 --> 00:17:29.466
reprehensible thing she does.

00:17:29.568 --> 00:17:32.986
Her anger goes unchecked, and to signify this, we see her pull out her tooth,

00:17:33.008 --> 00:17:36.090
which we'd seen her grinding throughout the movie as her anger grew.

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:39.454
After noticing Samuel spying from the top of the stairs, she chases after

00:17:39.492 --> 00:17:42.174
him and he locks himself away in his room, causing her to turn into

00:17:42.212 --> 00:17:45.306
something almost demonic as she climbs the wall trying to kick in the door

00:17:45.338 --> 00:17:46.542
and screams, let me in.

00:17:46.596 --> 00:17:49.326
The same phrase used by The Babadook in the children's book.

00:17:49.428 --> 00:17:52.366
Speaking of that book, after scaring him with it for the first time, she had

00:17:52.388 --> 00:17:55.714
to calm him down by reading him a fairy tale, and she uses similar tactics here.

00:17:55.762 --> 00:18:01.220
-[Amelia] You little pig. Six years old and you’re still wetting yourself.

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:02.222
By calling him a little pig.

00:18:02.222 --> 00:18:05.524
She's calling back to the Big Bad Wolf story that she read him earlier.

00:18:05.524 --> 00:18:08.332
Funny enough, I made the Jack Torrance comparison earlier

00:18:08.332 --> 00:18:11.450
and he too, used the Big Bad Wolf to taunt his own victims.

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:16.058
-[Jack Torrance] Little pigs, little pigs, let me come in.

00:18:16.144 --> 00:18:18.966
-[Jack Torrance] Not by the hair on your chinny chin chin.

00:18:19.078 --> 00:18:25.142
-[Jack Torrance] Then I'll huff and I'll puff and I'll blow your house in.

00:18:25.216 --> 00:18:29.070
Samuel throws a firecracker at her feet to try to escape, and she mockingly sings.

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:32.667
-[Amelia] Run, run, run as fast as you can.

00:18:32.667 --> 00:18:33.681
 Another fairy tale reference.

00:18:34.026 --> 00:18:37.038
The chase would go on for a while longer, including an interruption from a

00:18:37.044 --> 00:18:37.638
concerned Mrs. Roach,

00:18:37.674 --> 00:18:40.946
and it would end with Sam, who has nowhere else to run, striking his

00:18:40.968 --> 00:18:42.290
mom and knocking her out.

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:45.346
I guess his fighting words earlier were not so unwarranted after all.

00:18:45.368 --> 00:18:46.498
This kid's the real deal.

00:18:46.584 --> 00:18:49.982
When she awakes, she finds herself in one more fairy tale like scenario,

00:18:50.046 --> 00:18:53.410
tied down with rope in the basement, like the giant in Gulliver's Travels.

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:56.278
I think the references to all these fairy tales are to highlight how

00:18:56.284 --> 00:18:59.906
The Babadook draws upon elements from fairy tales, which often contain moral

00:18:59.938 --> 00:19:02.794
lessons and allegorical elements, which is what we're supposed to be looking

00:19:02.832 --> 00:19:03.850
for here as well.

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:07.498
And it seems by this point, sam very much understands the true nature of The Babadook,

00:19:07.504 --> 00:19:10.000
because he says this.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:13.194
-[Sam] I know you don't love me. The Babadook won't let you.

00:19:13.312 --> 00:19:14.806
-[Sam] But I love you, Mum.

00:19:14.918 --> 00:19:16.540
-[Sam] And I always will.

00:19:16.673 --> 00:19:19.046
-[Sam] You let it in. You have to get it out!

00:19:19.168 --> 00:19:22.542
Her hands get free, and The Babadook forces her to strangle her own son.

00:19:22.596 --> 00:19:25.674
But she seems to get a hold of herself and vomits out a black substance,

00:19:25.722 --> 00:19:28.110
essentially expelling the creature's control over her body.

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:31.566
And while this is a good first step, Sam reminds her, you can't get rid of

00:19:31.588 --> 00:19:32.202
The Babadook.

00:19:32.266 --> 00:19:34.754
And then he's Air Yeeted up the stairs.

00:19:34.922 --> 00:19:38.406
I still remember seeing this movie for the first time in 2014 and rolling my

00:19:38.428 --> 00:19:39.382
eyes when I saw this.

00:19:39.436 --> 00:19:42.102
I think this was actually the first time that I ever coined this as a common

00:19:42.156 --> 00:19:43.314
phenomena in horror movies.

00:19:43.362 --> 00:19:45.110
I don't know if I called it Air Yeeting yet.

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:48.326
Not sure if Yeet had entered the lexicon at that point, but I definitely had

00:19:48.348 --> 00:19:51.494
seen The Conjuring and Carrie and Insidious 2 the previous year.

00:19:51.532 --> 00:19:54.358
And I was pretty sick of seeing characters get pulled out of thin air in

00:19:54.364 --> 00:19:55.186
every horror movie.

00:19:55.218 --> 00:19:57.766
And The Babadook was just the one that broke me because it didn't need to do that.

00:19:57.788 --> 00:20:00.494
There seems to be a lot of talk about The Babadook

00:20:00.532 --> 00:20:03.774
being so intelligent and elevated, but really when you break it down, it's the same

00:20:03.812 --> 00:20:07.454
flickering lights, creepy phone calls, possession, black vomit, moving furniture 

00:20:07.492 --> 00:20:10.798
and Air Yeeting that was going on in every paranormal movie at the time.

00:20:10.804 --> 00:20:12.314
I'm not a Babadook hater.

00:20:12.362 --> 00:20:15.118
I like that there's substance to the movie, but don't act like it reinvented

00:20:15.134 --> 00:20:15.986
the genre or anything.

00:20:16.088 --> 00:20:18.290
Also, this one, Air Yeet, is kind of funny.

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:23.118
I'm so immature.

00:20:23.214 --> 00:20:28.318
As the beds start shaking, Amelia screams into the dark void on the other side of the room, asking...

00:20:28.318 --> 00:20:30.109
-[Amelia] What do you want?

00:20:30.109 --> 00:20:33.826
For the first time, she acknowledges the creature as something more than a figment of her son's imagination.

00:20:33.858 --> 00:20:37.846
And in doing so, she acknowledges the problem growing inside of her.

00:20:37.868 --> 00:20:40.779
And this takes her to...

00:20:42.352 --> 00:20:44.758
Once more, Oskar emerges from the shadows.

00:20:44.854 --> 00:20:48.214
This time he begins repeating his lines from right before the car crash.

00:20:48.262 --> 00:20:51.818
And Amelia is forced to see and live through the tragedy all over again.

00:20:51.904 --> 00:20:55.086
But this time, instead of going straight into denial, she gets mad and

00:20:55.108 --> 00:20:57.738
confronts the monster festering in the darkness.

00:20:57.914 --> 00:21:01.886
-[Amelia] If you touch my son again, I'll kill you.

00:21:01.988 --> 00:21:04.302
Yeah. Don't ever talk to me or my son again.

00:21:04.436 --> 00:21:07.714
Although it seems to have reached its final form, her ferocity seems to

00:21:07.752 --> 00:21:11.138
weaken The Babadook and it shrinks back into the darkness, leaving only the

00:21:11.144 --> 00:21:13.102
hat and jacket which fall to the floor.

00:21:13.166 --> 00:21:16.786
Upon touching these clothing articles, the entity screeches at her, scampers

00:21:16.818 --> 00:21:18.950
down to the basement and locks itself in.

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:22.658
And from this point, although she seems to have overcome The Babadook,

00:21:22.754 --> 00:21:25.320
that's far from the whole story.

00:21:25.763 --> 00:21:28.520
(IMPACT)

00:21:31.032 --> 00:21:33.839
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:38.170
I withheld the rest of the story, from the physical Babadook book until now

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:41.876
for probably the same reason that the end of the book is never shown in the film.

00:21:41.876 --> 00:21:43.082
It would kind of spoil it.

00:21:43.136 --> 00:21:47.166
You cannot get rid of me, dare to look me in my face, try to put me in my place.

00:21:47.188 --> 00:21:51.374
I will cause you so much strife, but you might just get out with your life,

00:21:51.412 --> 00:21:53.198
whether adult or child,

00:21:53.284 --> 00:21:57.154
Best to give me a home, put the welcome mat out with a room of my own,

00:21:57.192 --> 00:21:59.582
And accept that I'm here and from you I have grown.

00:21:59.646 --> 00:22:02.686
Keep me smaller in size I might leave you alone.

00:22:02.798 --> 00:22:04.434
I only said might.

00:22:04.552 --> 00:22:07.986
You can't get rid of The Babadook, so the only solution is to learn to live

00:22:08.008 --> 00:22:09.090
with it in a healthy way.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:12.470
After that final night, we see Amelia attempting to do just that.

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:15.398
She explains to the social workers that they celebrated Sam's birthday on

00:22:15.404 --> 00:22:16.822
the actual day for the first time.

00:22:16.876 --> 00:22:20.086
Only now she's being very transparent about why they never did it before.

00:22:20.188 --> 00:22:23.734
She can also be seen planting black roses, a universal symbol of mourning,

00:22:23.782 --> 00:22:25.942
in the backyard over where the dog is now buried.

00:22:26.006 --> 00:22:28.794
Which shows us that this time she's dealing with the grief head on.

00:22:28.832 --> 00:22:32.534
Instead of avoiding it, like she did for seven years with Oskar.

00:22:32.582 --> 00:22:35.950
Her and Sam collect a bowl of worms from the spot and she instructs him to

00:22:36.020 --> 00:22:38.894
stay inside while she takes the bowl into the basement, where The Babadook

00:22:38.932 --> 00:22:41.210
is locked up and seemingly feeds him using the bowl of worms.

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:44.746
The short film Monster has a similar ending, where the mother periodically

00:22:44.778 --> 00:22:47.322
feeds the monster a glass of milk to keep it satiated.

00:22:47.386 --> 00:22:49.514
So what is the significance of the switch to worms

00:22:49.514 --> 00:22:51.656
as the snack of choice for The Babadook?

00:22:51.656 --> 00:22:53.026
I think it has to do with the journey that

00:22:53.048 --> 00:22:56.334
Amelia has gone through to reach this point during the climax.

00:22:56.462 --> 00:22:57.998
No, not that climax.

00:22:58.094 --> 00:23:01.142
She was so far gone that she killed her dog and nearly her son.

00:23:01.196 --> 00:23:03.986
If you look closely, the worms are picked right from above the dog's gravesite,

00:23:04.018 --> 00:23:07.506
so she seems to be using them as a motivation to keep her grief

00:23:07.538 --> 00:23:10.438
in check by taking some time to acknowledge it every now and then so it

00:23:10.444 --> 00:23:13.434
doesn't grow and fester into a monster that she cannot control.

00:23:13.552 --> 00:23:16.922
Amelia never fully defeats The Babadook, but this is a good first step

00:23:16.976 --> 00:23:19.830
towards taking some of its power and making it more manageable.

00:23:19.910 --> 00:23:23.402
The film as a whole plays as a very lonely, isolating experience

00:23:23.456 --> 00:23:25.182
because only her and the son encounter it.

00:23:25.236 --> 00:23:29.162
If you're not living in that house, it would be difficult to ever truly understand.

00:23:29.162 --> 00:23:31.598
Jennifer Kent's next movie, The Nightingale, also features a mother

00:23:31.604 --> 00:23:34.846
who loses her husband and baby at the beginning of the movie,

00:23:34.868 --> 00:23:35.882
and she sets out for revenge.

00:23:35.946 --> 00:23:39.314
Like The Babadook, there's no real catharsis there's only learning to better

00:23:39.352 --> 00:23:41.058
deal with the circumstances at hand.

00:23:41.144 --> 00:23:45.074
Amelia is at the beginning of a road to healing, a road that there is no

00:23:45.112 --> 00:23:46.946
guarantee she'll make it down.

00:23:47.048 --> 00:23:50.466
But just as Amelia's battle is not over, neither is your viewing session.

00:23:50.498 --> 00:23:53.990
I've got more Horror History analyses lined up in that playlist on the left.

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:56.982
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors every week.

00:23:57.036 --> 00:24:00.710
Ring the Deathbell for all notifications, and I'll see you in the next one,

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:02.370
assuming we both survive.

